    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-333643",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$\n$y=-x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-2x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217341/assets/450d43a4cbf6dad8d54edbb66fc4a6e44e491290e1dc5d1303057fb0eb7928a7.png)",
